  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework has only been tested in a controlled indoor pilot, and domain-level interpretation requires further validation across diverse real-world settings.",
      "what": "Researchers developed the NeuroDesign/NeuroArchitecture Index (NDIX), a framework measuring six experiential dimensions of the built environment: naturalness, sensory, cognitive, emotional, social, and safety and accessibility. A self-report tool converts user experience into percentage scores across these domains, which was initially pilot-tested in a controlled indoor environment to detect environmental differences.",
      "why": "Existing building certifications evaluate physical features but fail to quantify how specific spatial properties directly alter human physiological and psychological responses."
    }
